                <div><p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800">RWAHQ Editorial</p><p class="text-xs text-gray-400">Independent Research</p></div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400 ml-auto">
                <time datetime="2025-05-28" class="flex items-center gap-1"><i data-lucide="calendar" class="w-3.5 h-3.5"></i> May 28, 2025</time>
                <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><i data-lucide="eye" class="w-3.5 h-3.5"></i> 15.7K</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </header>

          <div class="prose-custom px-6 sm:px-10 py-10">

            <blockquote class="not-prose border-l-4 border-purple-500 pl-5 py-2 my-8 bg-purple-50/50 rounded-r-lg">
              <p class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-700 italic leading-relaxed">"Most 'DeFi explained' articles read like marketing material for the protocol they're describing. We tried to write the one we actually needed when we first got confused looking at Aave's interface at 2am on a Tuesday."</p>
            </blockquote>

            <h2 id="the-basics" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-2 mb-4">The Basics: Lending Without Banks</h2>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Let's strip away all the crypto jargon for a second. At its core, DeFi lending is pretty simple:</p>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>You have money. Someone else needs money. You lend it to them through software instead of a bank.</strong> They pay you interest. The software holds their collateral in escrow. If they don't pay back, the software automatically sells their collateral to make you whole. No loan officer, no branch visit, no three-day wait.</p>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">That's literally it. Everything else is implementation details. But those implementation details matter a lot more than most guides let on — especially if you're actually considering putting money in.</p>

            <div class="not-prose bg-gradient-to-br from-purple-50 to-indigo-50 border border-purple-200/60 rounded-xl p-6 mb-6">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <div class="w-12 h-12 bg-purple-100 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0"><i data-lucide="cog" class="w-6 h-6 text-purple-600"></i></div>
                <div>
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">⚙️ The Core Loop</h3>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">You deposit USDC into a lending pool on Aave. The protocol aggregates all deposits into one big pot. Borrowers take USDC from that pot and post ETH as collateral. A Chainlink oracle feeds the ETH price. If the collateral value drops below a threshold, anyone can liquidate the position to protect lenders. Interest rates float based on utilization — more borrowing pushes rates up, attracting more deposits.</p>
                  <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-1.5">
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-purple-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Supply: deposit USDC → earn variable APY (3-6%)</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-purple-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Borrow: post $150 ETH collateral → get $100 USDC loan</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-purple-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Liquidation: if ETH drops, protocol auto-sells to protect lenders</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucive="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-purple-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Settlement: instant, on-chain, 24/7</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">That's the loop. Now let's look at what makes each protocol handle differently — because "DeFi lending" isn't actually one thing. It's four or five different approaches with real tradeoffs that nobody explains clearly.</p>

            <h2 id="the-numbers" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Numbers (From Actual On-Chain Data)</h2>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">I pulled these from <a href="https://defillama.com/categories/lending" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">DeFi Llama's lending category</a> on May 28, 2025. These aren't estimates — they're what's actually on-chain right now.</p>

            <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
              <div class="stat-card bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
                <p class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-purple-600 font-mono">$12.1B</p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1.5 uppercase tracking-wider font-medium">Total Lending TVL</p>
                <p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1">DeFi Llama, May 2025</p>
              </div>
              <div class="stat-card bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
                <p class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-purple-600 font-mono">$12B</p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1.5 uppercase tracking-wider font-medium">Aave v3 + v4 Combined</p>
                <p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1">Single protocol</p>
              </div>
              <div class="stat-card bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
                <p class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-purple-600 font-mono">$780M</p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1.5 uppercase tracking-wider font-medium">Supply-Side Yield Paid Out (Cumulative)</p>
                <p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1">All lending protocols combined</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">$12.1 billion sounds like a big number until you realize that traditional money markets handle roughly $630 trillion in daily transactions. DeFi lending is less than 0.002% of traditional volumes. The growth is real — DeFi Llama shows lending TVL roughly doubled from mid-2024 — but the starting point was tiny.</p>

            <div class="ad-slot my-8 py-4 border-y border-gray-100 flex justify-center"><div class="w-full max-w-[336px] h-[280px] bg-gray-50 flex items-center justify-center border border-gray-200 rounded-lg"><span class="text-gray-400 text-xs font-mono text-center leading-relaxed">ADSENSE / MONETAG<br>(336×280)</span></div></div>

            <h2 id="aave" class="texttext-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Aave: The Giant</h2>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Aave is the biggest name in DeFi lending for a reason — they were first to market with the basic pool model in 2020, and they've been iterating aggressively ever since. As of May 2025, Aave v3 (and the newer v4 on some chains) holds roughly <strong>$12 billion in total TVL</strong> across Ethereum, Arbitrum, Polygon, Optimism, Avalanche, and others.</p>

            <div class="not-prose bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-purple-200 hover:shadow-sm transition-all mb-6">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                  <div class="w-10 h-10 bg-purple-700 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-white font-bold text-xs">AAVE</div>
                  <div><h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">Aave v3/v4</h3><p class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono">Ethereum + 9 more chains</p></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-xs bg-green-50 text-green-700 font-semibold px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full border border-green-200">TVL: ~$12B</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">The biggest lending protocol by a wide margin. Supports flash loans, multi-collateral, RWA collateral on some deployments, and the GHO stablecoin ecosystem. The main downside: take rates are higher than competitors because of overhead from all those features most borrowers don't use.</p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">What actually makes Aave powerful isn't the lending mechanism — it's the network effects. Being the biggest means the deepest liquidity, the most integrations (GHO, Spark Protocol, dozens of DeFi apps using Aave as a backend), and the most audited codebase. If you're building something that needs a lending backend, Aave is usually the default choice. It's not the cheapest option — but it's the safest bet.</p>

            <h2 id="compound" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compound: The Lean Competitor</h2>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Compound has been around almost as long as Aave but took a very different approach with v4 in mid-2024. Instead of simple liquidity pools, they introduced "Suppliers" — abstracted yield strategies that auto-route your deposit to the highest-yielding market.</p>

            <div class="not-prose bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-purple-200 hover:shadow-sm transition-all mb-6">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                  <div class="w-10 h-10 bg-green-600 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-white font-bold text-xs">COMP</div>
                  <div><h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">Compound v4</h3><p class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono">Ethereum + 8 more chains</p></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-xs bg-green-50 text-green-700 font-semibold px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full border border-green-200">TVL: ~$2.4B</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Compound v4's "Suppliers" let you create custom yield strategies without understanding the underlying pool. The protocol takes a lower fee than Aave (0.1% vs 0.15%), and the routing is more capital-efficient. But the TVL is much smaller, which means thinner liquidity. It's the "efficiency play" versus Aave's "scale play."</p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">We think Compound is technically more interesting than Aave — the Supplier architecture is genuinely novel. But in practice, most people use Aave because that's what their tools support and that's where the liquidity is. Being "better" doesn't help much if nobody shows up to use your product.</p>

            <h2 id="morpho" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Morpho Blue: The Efficiency Play</h2>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><a href="https://app.morpho.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Morpho Blue</a> takes a radically different approach. Instead of routing everything through pools, it matches lenders and borrowers directly. No pool intermediary means almost 100% of borrow interest goes to the lender. The take rate is under 2% compared to 10-15% for traditional pool-based lending.</p>

            <div class="not-prose bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-purple-200 hover:shadow-sm transition-all mb-6">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                  <div class="w-10 h-10 bg-blue-500 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center text-white font-bold text-xs">MORPH</div>
                  <div><h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">Morpho Blue</h3><p class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono">Ethereum, Base, Optimism</p></div>
                </div>
                <span class="text-xs bg-green-50 text-green-700 font-semibold px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full border border-green-200">TVL: ~$850M</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Highest capital efficiency in DeFi lending. Best for large, uniform positions (WBTC, WETH). The tradeoff: fewer supported assets, higher oracle dependency, and if the oracle gets manipulated, the whole system breaks faster because there's no pool buffer.</p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">Morpho is the darling of DeFi power users who do the math and optimize relentlessly. For normal users, it's probably overkill — and the risk profile is arguably worse. But it's worth understanding because it represents where lending is heading technically, even if it doesn't become the default.</p>

            <div class="ad-slot my-8 py-4 border-y border-gray-100 flex justify-center"><div class="w-full max-w-[336px] h-[280px] bg-gray-50 flex items-center justify-center border border-gray-200 rounded-lg"><span class="text-gray-400 text-xs font-mono text-center leading-relaxed">ADSENSE / MONETAG<br>(336×280)</span></div></div>

            <h2 id="comparison" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Side-by-Side: What Actually Differs</h2>

            <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
              <table class="table-defi w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
                <thead>
                  <tr class="bg-purple-600 text-white">
                    <th class="p-4 font-semibold">Aspect</th>
                    <th class="p-4 font-semibold">Aave</th>
                    <th class="p-4 font-semibold">Compound v4</th>
                    <th class="p-4 font-semibold">Morpho Blue</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                  <tr>
                    <td class="p-4 font-semibold text-gray-800 border-b border-gray-100">TVL</td>
                    <td class="p-4 border-b border-gray-100 font-mono">~$12B</td>
                    <td class="p-4 border-b border-gray-100 font-mono">~$2.4B</td>
                    <td class="p-4 border-b border-gray-100 font-mono">~$850M</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="p-4 font-semibold text-gray-800 border-b border-gray-100">Supply APY (USDC)</td>
                    <td class="p-4 border-b border-gray-100 font-mono">3.2–5.8%</td>
                    <td class="p-4 border-b border-gray-100 font-mono text-green-700 font-medium">4.1–6.3%</td>
                    <td class="p-4 border-b border-gray-100 font-mono text-green-700 font-medium">5.5–7.2%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="p-4 font-semibold text-gray-800 border-b border-gray-100">Take Rate</td>
