            <h2 id="rwa-in-lending" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where RWA Collateral Fits In</h2>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">This is the newest and most hyped angle — using tokenized Treasuries as collateral on DeFi lending protocols. It sounds incredible. And in limited ways, it actually works.</p>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">On Aave (via their Spark Protocol deployment) and on certain configurations, you can now deposit BlackRock's <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/products/usb-institutional-digital-liquidity-fund" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">BUIDL tokens</a> as collateral to borrow stablecoins. As of May 2025, roughly $1.1 billion in tokenized assets are being used as collateral across various protocols according to <a href="https://rwa.xyz" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">rwa.xyz</a>.</p>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">The real use case: institutional treasuries already yield 4–5%. By borrowing against them on-chain, you can access liquidity without selling the underlying asset. The effective borrow cost drops to roughly 2–3% after subtracting the yield you're already earning. This is genuinely useful for treasury management. But it's still early — the infrastructure is clunky, the user experience is bad, and most institutions are still in "pilot" mode rather than production.</p>

            <div class="not-prose bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200/60 rounded-xl p-6 mb-6">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                <div class="w-12 h-12 bg-blue-100 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0"><i data-lucide="landmark" class="w-6 h-6 text-blue-600"></i></div>
                <div>
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">How RWA Collateral Flow Actually Works</h3>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">Buy tokenized T-Bills on-chain → deposit to Aave as collateral → borrow USDC at ~6–7% → deploy capital elsewhere → keep earning the T-Bill yield. Effective cost: ~2–3%. This actually makes sense for treasury management. But the UX is still terrible and most institutions haven't touched it in production.</p>
                  <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-1.5">
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-blue-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Real yield from the T-Bill partially offsets borrowing cost</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-blue-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> No need to sell the underlying asset to access liquidity</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-blue-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Works on Aave and Spark Protocol today (pilot mode)</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="x" class="w-4 h-4 text-blue-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> UX and tooling still early — not ready for mainstream institutional use</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="the-risks" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Risks Nobody Likes to Talk About</h2>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">Every "RWA is safe" take should make you more skeptical, not less. The risk hasn't disappeared — it's just shifted form.</p>

            <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i data-lucide="bug" class="w-5 h-5 text-red-500"></i><h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Smart Contract Risk</h3></div>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Over $1.2 billion has been lost to lending exploits since 2020 according to <a href="https://rekt.news" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Rekt.news</a>. The code is audited, but audits miss things. The bigger the TVL grows, the bigger the target becomes.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i data-lucide="trending-down" class="w-5 h-5 text-red-500"></i><h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Liquidation Cascades</h3></div>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">When markets crash simultaneously, correlated collateral values all drop at once. Liquidators compete to sell, prices crash further, causing more liquidations. Aave accumulated roughly $14M in bad debt from the August 2024 ETH crash. This is structural — it will happen again.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i data-lucide="radio" class="w-5 h-5 text-red-500"></i><h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Oracle Risk</h3></div>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">All on-chain lending depends on price oracles (Chainlink, Pyth). If an oracle fails or gets manipulated, the protocol might value your collateral at $0 when it should be worth millions. This has happened repeatedly. It's the systemic risk nobody wants to discuss.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i data-lucide="shield-off" class="w-5 h-5 text-red-500"></i><h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Governance Attacks</h3></div>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Protocol changes are decided by token holders voting. Large token holders can theoretically push through changes that benefit themselves at the expense of smaller holders. This has happened — quietly — the effects are subtle but real.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-5 my-6 flex items-start gap-3">
              <i data-lucide="alert-triangle" class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-500 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5"></i>
              <p class="text-sm text-amber-800 leading-relaxed"><strong>The bottom line:</strong> DeFi lending is real and useful, but it's not safe. It's not some magical risk-free yield machine. The people telling you otherwise are either selling you something or haven't lost enough money yet. Neither is a good reason to skip doing your own research.</p>
            </div>

            <h2 id="what-next" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What's Actually Next</h2>

            <div class="not-prose space-y-3 mb-6">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-4 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                <span class="w-7 h-7 bg-purple-100 text-purple-700 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5">1</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800">Undercollateralized Lending</p>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-0.5">Maple Finance, Goldfinch, Centrifuge — they lend without requiring 150% over-collateral by using real-world legal enforcement. Currently ~$6B in active loans. Riskier but growing fast.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-4 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                <span class="w-7 h-7 bg-purple-100 text-purple-700 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5">2</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800">Cross-Chain Lending</p>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-0.5">LayerZero and Axelar enable borrowing on one chain against collateral on another. Sounds simple. In practice, cross-chain state is hard and the security model is still debated.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-4 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                <span class="w-7 h-7 bg-purple-100 text-purple-700 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5">3</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800">Fixed-Rate Lending</p>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-0.5">Pendle Finance lets you lock in a fixed interest rate by splitting assets into principal and yield tokens. Lenders buy "Principal Tokens" for predictable returns. Interesting but complex — still niche.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="conclusion" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What We Actually Think</h2>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">DeFi lending is one of the few areas in crypto where the product-market fit is proven — people genuinely use these protocols to borrow and lend. The problem isn't adoption. The problem is that everyone writes about it like it's a revolution when it's really just a moderately better plumbing system for something that already worked.</p>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The protocols that will win aren't the ones with the best tokenomics or the most hype. They'll be the ones that don't require users to become crypto-literate to use them. That means better UX, better documentation, better integration with traditional finance workflows. Aave and Compound are both moving in this direction.</p>

            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">And the protocols that lose will be the ones that keep treating "education" as marketing — publishing explainer articles like this one, while their actual product remains confusing. We're aware of the irony.</p>

            <div class="not-prose mt-10 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-purple-900 to-indigo-900 rounded-xl text-center">
              <p class="text-purple-300 text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mb-4">Sponsored Content</p>
              <div class="bg-white/10 w-full h-36 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center border border-white/10"><span class="text-white/40 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider">Native Banner Ads</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">FAQ</h2>

            <div class="not-prose space-y-3 mb-8" id="faq-container">
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">How does DeFi lending actually work?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">You deposit crypto into a smart contract pool. Other users borrow from that pool and pay interest. The interest rate adjusts automatically based on how much of the pool is borrowed. Your deposit earns yield. Borrowers post collateral worth more than they borrow. If the collateral value drops too low, anyone can liquidate it to protect lenders. All automated, 24/7, no bank needed.</div></div></div>
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">What's the actual yield I can earn?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">As of May 2025, depositing USDC on Aave earns roughly 3-6% APY (variable). On Morpho, you might get 4-7% because there's no pool take. Compound is somewhere in between. These are real yields from real on-chain activity — but they fluctuate a lot and can drop sharply during market stress.</div></div></div>
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">Is it safe to lend on DeFi?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">"Safe" is the wrong framing. It's "less risky than uncollateralized lending" or "safer than some CEX operations." But not safe. Over $1.2B has been lost to exploits. Liquidation cascades are real. Oracle failures are real. Smart contract bugs are real. It's safer than holding funds on a centralized exchange — but that's a low bar. Do your own assessment before depositing large amounts.</div></div></div>
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">Why does everyone say Aave is better than Compound?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">Because it has more TVL, more chains, and more third-party integrations. For a builder, Aave is the safe default — you won't hit dead-end compatibility issues. For a power user optimizing for yield, Compound's lower take rates and Supplier architecture may actually perform better. Neither is objectively better — they optimize for different things.</div></div></div>
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">Can I lose all my money?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">Yes. Smart contract bugs, oracle failures, and liquidation cascades can all result in total loss. However, over-collateralization (typically 110-150%) provides a buffer. The $14M Aave bad debt from August 2024 hurt but didn't wipe out all depositors — most got back 70-90% of their funds after liquidation. Your risk scales with how much you deposit and where.</div></div></div>
            </div>

